1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,557
Everyone in the AI race is
trying to pick the winner.

2
00:00:03,597 --> 00:00:08,132
One company is positioned to profit almost
no matter which chip designer comes out

3
00:00:08,172 --> 00:00:09,170
on top.

4
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:13,560
Nvidia and AMD are fierce
rivals in AI chips.

5
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:17,635
Yet their own annual reports name the
same manufacturer for their most advanced

6
00:00:17,675 --> 00:00:18,697
chips.

7
00:00:19,447 --> 00:00:21,183
Its name isn't printed on the chips,

8
00:00:21,223 --> 00:00:23,227
and most people have never heard of it.

9
00:00:23,267 --> 00:00:25,949
But the AI industry depends on it.

10
00:00:26,699 --> 00:00:28,691
It's called TSMC,

11
00:00:28,731 --> 00:00:33,439
the Taiwan Semiconductor
Manufacturing Company. In 2025,

12
00:00:33,479 --> 00:00:39,934
its revenue reached about 122 billion
dollars, up 36 percent in a single year.

13
00:00:43,284 --> 00:00:44,927
Follow a single AI chip.

14
00:00:44,967 --> 00:00:47,749
Companies like Nvidia and AMD design it.

15
00:00:47,789 --> 00:00:50,314
But a design is only a blueprint.

16
00:00:50,354 --> 00:00:54,290
Someone has to turn it into
billions of transistors on silicon.

17
00:00:55,040 --> 00:00:58,181
AMD's annual report puts it plainly.

18
00:00:58,221 --> 00:01:04,474
We rely on TSMC for the production of all
wafers for microprocessor and GPU products

19
00:01:04,514 --> 00:01:07,486
at 7 nanometer or smaller nodes.

20
00:01:08,236 --> 00:01:13,711
Nvidia's annual report names two
manufacturers: TSMC and Samsung.

21
00:01:13,751 --> 00:01:17,821
And
when Nvidia celebrated the first Blackwell wafer produced in the United States,

22
00:01:17,861 --> 00:01:23,097
in October 2025, it came out
of TSMC's factory in Arizona.

23
00:01:23,847 --> 00:01:25,548
That's the foundry model.

24
00:01:25,588 --> 00:01:29,031
TSMC doesn't sell AI
chips under its own name.

25
00:01:29,071 --> 00:01:31,771
It manufactures chips that
other companies design,

26
00:01:31,811 --> 00:01:33,559
and it competes for their orders,

27
00:01:33,599 --> 00:01:35,178
not for their customers.

28
00:01:35,928 --> 00:01:36,805
By that measure,

29
00:01:36,845 --> 00:01:38,849
the gap is enormous.

30
00:01:38,889 --> 00:01:44,677
The research firm TrendForce estimates
TSMC took 70.4 percent of global foundry

31
00:01:44,717 --> 00:01:47,858
revenue in the last quarter of 2025.

32
00:01:47,898 --> 00:01:52,879
Samsung's foundry business, in
second place, took 7.1 percent.

33
00:01:53,629 --> 00:01:55,238
In dollars, that quarter:

34
00:01:55,278 --> 00:01:58,953
about 33.7 billion for TSMC.

35
00:01:58,993 --> 00:02:02,691
About 3.4 billion for
Samsung's foundry business.

36
00:02:02,731 --> 00:02:06,493
About 2.5 billion for China's SMIC.

37
00:02:07,243 --> 00:02:09,223
That matters for the AI race.

38
00:02:09,263 --> 00:02:11,557
If AMD takes ground from Nvidia,

39
00:02:11,597 --> 00:02:15,881
the orders for those chips are still
likely to land at the same factory.

40
00:02:16,631 --> 00:02:21,432
You can see this in TSMC's
own numbers. In 2025,

41
00:02:21,472 --> 00:02:22,895
high-performance computing,

42
00:02:22,935 --> 00:02:25,728
the category that
includes AI accelerators,

43
00:02:25,768 --> 00:02:28,143
made up 58 percent of its revenue,

44
00:02:28,183 --> 00:02:30,842
and grew 48 percent in a year.

45
00:02:31,591 --> 00:02:32,433
Smartphones,

46
00:02:32,473 --> 00:02:37,565
the business that built the modern
TSMC, were 29 percent. Everything else,

47
00:02:37,605 --> 00:02:41,715
from cars to connected
devices, about 13 percent.

48
00:02:45,065 --> 00:02:47,614
There's an old saying about gold rushes.

49
00:02:47,654 --> 00:02:50,644
The surest way to get rich
isn't digging for gold.

50
00:02:50,684 --> 00:02:52,356
It's selling the shovels.

51
00:02:53,106 --> 00:02:56,549
Nvidia is often called
the shovel seller of AI,

52
00:02:56,589 --> 00:02:59,243
and it has been spectacularly successful:

53
00:02:59,283 --> 00:03:05,645
nearly 216 billion dollars in
revenue in its fiscal year 2026.

54
00:03:06,395 --> 00:03:08,898
And Nvidia keeps more of each dollar.

55
00:03:08,938 --> 00:03:11,521
Its gross margin was 71 percent,

56
00:03:11,561 --> 00:03:15,864
even after a one-off charge
of 4.5 billion dollars.

57
00:03:15,904 --> 00:03:18,051
TSMC's was 60 percent.

58
00:03:18,802 --> 00:03:21,432
That's because they sit at
different points in the chain.

59
00:03:21,472 --> 00:03:24,880
Nvidia designs and sells
complete chips and systems.

60
00:03:24,920 --> 00:03:27,353
TSMC is paid to manufacture them.

61
00:03:27,393 --> 00:03:30,644
Part of what Nvidia spends, TSMC earns.

62
00:03:31,394 --> 00:03:34,825
Nvidia has to keep winning
to keep those margins.

63
00:03:34,865 --> 00:03:38,750
TSMC benefits as long as demand
for advanced chips keeps growing,

64
00:03:38,790 --> 00:03:40,410
whoever designs them.

65
00:03:40,450 --> 00:03:41,768
That's an advantage.

66
00:03:41,808 --> 00:03:44,258
It isn't a guarantee.

67
00:03:45,008 --> 00:03:47,260
The dependence goes beyond the silicon.

68
00:03:47,910 --> 00:03:51,899
Modern AI chips need an advanced
packaging step called CoWoS,

69
00:03:51,939 --> 00:03:55,503
which places the processor
right next to high-speed memory.

70
00:03:56,253 --> 00:03:59,348
By one estimate from the
research group Epoch AI,

71
00:03:59,388 --> 00:04:04,119
the four largest AI chip designers used
around 90 percent of the world's CoWoS

72
00:04:04,159 --> 00:04:06,795
capacity in 2025.

73
00:04:07,545 --> 00:04:13,461
Epoch AI concludes that in 2025, AI
chip production was held back mainly by

74
00:04:13,501 --> 00:04:15,226
packaging and memory,

75
00:04:15,266 --> 00:04:17,281
not by chip design.

76
00:04:17,321 --> 00:04:22,406
Whoever controls that capacity has real
influence over how many AI chips get made.

77
00:04:25,756 --> 00:04:28,630
But even TSMC depends on someone.

78
00:04:28,670 --> 00:04:34,516
To print the tiniest features on a chip,
it uses extreme ultraviolet lithography.

79
00:04:34,556 --> 00:04:37,790
And only one company in the
world sells those machines:

80
00:04:37,830 --> 00:04:41,220
ASML, in the Netherlands.

81
00:04:41,970 --> 00:04:43,799
ASML's most advanced model,

82
00:04:43,839 --> 00:04:45,819
called High-NA EUV,

83
00:04:45,859 --> 00:04:51,032
reportedly costs around
380 to 400 million dollars.

84
00:04:51,072 --> 00:04:52,698
For a single machine.

85
00:04:53,448 --> 00:04:56,020
So the structure looks
like this. At the top,

86
00:04:56,060 --> 00:04:58,969
the AI products everyone
talks about. Below them,

87
00:04:59,009 --> 00:05:00,943
the chip designers. Below them,

88
00:05:00,983 --> 00:05:04,542
the manufacturers, led
by TSMC. And underneath,

89
00:05:04,582 --> 00:05:07,519
lithography machines from
a single Dutch company.

90
00:05:10,869 --> 00:05:13,133
Now the part that worries governments.

91
00:05:13,783 --> 00:05:19,595
A
2024 report by the Semiconductor Industry Association and Boston Consulting Group

92
00:05:19,635 --> 00:05:25,632
found
that in 2022, 69 percent of the world's wafer capacity for the most advanced

93
00:05:25,672 --> 00:05:26,596
logic chips,

94
00:05:26,636 --> 00:05:29,092
below 10 nanometers, was in Taiwan.

95
00:05:29,132 --> 00:05:31,303
The rest was in South Korea.

96
00:05:32,053 --> 00:05:38,340
The same report forecasts Taiwan's share
falling to 47 percent by 2032, if the

97
00:05:38,380 --> 00:05:41,382
factories announced since 2020 are built.

98
00:05:41,422 --> 00:05:43,895
That's a projection, not a result.

99
00:05:44,645 --> 00:05:49,806
Even so, a large part of the AI economy
rests on factories on one island, in an

100
00:05:49,846 --> 00:05:54,044
active earthquake zone, at the center
of one of the world's most sensitive

101
00:05:54,084 --> 00:05:55,883
geopolitical disputes.

102
00:05:56,633 --> 00:06:01,144
Which is why the map is starting
to change. In March 2025,

103
00:06:01,184 --> 00:06:07,715
TSMC said it expects its total investment
in the United States to reach 165 billion

104
00:06:07,755 --> 00:06:08,800
dollars.

105
00:06:09,551 --> 00:06:14,561
Its first Arizona factory entered
volume production in late 2024.

106
00:06:14,601 --> 00:06:19,809
But notice who is building
these factories: TSMC itself.

107
00:06:19,849 --> 00:06:23,199
Moving production closer to
customers reduces one risk.

108
00:06:23,239 --> 00:06:25,155
It doesn't change who
the industry depends on.

109
00:06:28,505 --> 00:06:29,847
So who profits?

110
00:06:29,887 --> 00:06:34,398
TSMC and ASML are positioned
to profit from AI chip demand,

111
00:06:34,438 --> 00:06:36,337
whichever designer wins.

112
00:06:36,377 --> 00:06:38,740
Who's under pressure? Rival foundries,

113
00:06:38,780 --> 00:06:43,737
competing for the roughly 30 percent
of foundry revenue TSMC doesn't take.

114
00:06:44,487 --> 00:06:49,683
But TSMC doesn't win
every order. In July 2025,

115
00:06:49,723 --> 00:06:55,488
Samsung signed a contract worth about 16.5
billion dollars to make Tesla's next AI

116
00:06:55,528 --> 00:06:56,301
chip.

117
00:06:56,341 --> 00:07:01,902
And Nvidia's own annual report lists
Samsung as a foundry, alongside TSMC.

118
00:07:02,652 --> 00:07:05,189
And who controls the future of AI?

119
00:07:05,229 --> 00:07:07,860
Not only the companies
with the smartest models.

120
00:07:07,900 --> 00:07:11,970
Also the few that control the bottlenecks
underneath them: the machines,

121
00:07:12,010 --> 00:07:13,015
the factories,

122
00:07:13,055 --> 00:07:14,773
and the packaging lines.

123
00:07:15,523 --> 00:07:19,454
And TSMC is betting heavily
that demand keeps growing.

124
00:07:19,494 --> 00:07:25,554
It expects to spend 52 to 56 billion
dollars on capital investment in 2026.

125
00:07:26,304 --> 00:07:29,306
Three things will decide
how this plays out.

126
00:07:29,346 --> 00:07:32,348
Whether AI demand justifies that spending.

127
00:07:32,388 --> 00:07:34,879
Whether packaging capacity keeps up.

128
00:07:34,919 --> 00:07:39,121
And whether rivals like Samsung win
more of the most advanced orders.

129
00:07:39,871 --> 00:07:42,165
That's the real risk in this business.

130
00:07:42,205 --> 00:07:45,613
TSMC doesn't need to know
who wins the AI race.

131
00:07:45,653 --> 00:07:47,905
But it does need the race to keep going.

132
00:07:48,655 --> 00:07:53,375
The most dependable money in AI may not
go to whoever builds the smartest model.

133
00:07:53,415 --> 00:07:56,963
It may go to whoever owns
the bottleneck. For now,

134
00:07:57,003 --> 00:07:59,986
that's a company most
people have never heard of.

135
00:08:00,737 --> 00:08:02,206
This is THE NEXT SHIFT.

136
00:08:02,246 --> 00:08:04,777
Understand tomorrow before it happens.
